        "headingId": 231,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 399,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 232,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58494,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58495,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The majority of cases of giant cell fibroblastoma arise in superficial soft tissues of the trunk, the groin and axillary region, and (more rarely) the extremities and the head and neck region {3565673; 17349565; 2804904; 12502925}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58496,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The affected patients usually report a slow-growing, painless cutaneous lesion that is most often plaque-like {17349565; 2804904}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58497,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Giant cell fibroblastoma is a rare neoplasm arising predominantly but not exclusively in children, with a mean age of 6&nbsp;years and a male predominance {17349565; 2804904}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58498,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58499,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "<p>Giant cell fibroblastoma and DFSP share chromosomal and molecular features, consisting of rearrangements of chromosomes&nbsp;17 and&nbsp;22 and formation of a chimeric gene that fuses <em>COL1A1</em> at 17q21.33 with <em>PDGFB</em> at 22q13.1 {8988177}. The breakpoint in <em>COL1A1</em> is variable between patients (from exon&nbsp;6 up to exon&nbsp;49), whereas the breakpoint in <em>PDGFB</em> is consistently located in intron&nbsp;1 {12661001}. There is no evidence of a correlation between a specific breakpoint within <em>COL1A1</em> and either giant cell fibroblastoma or classic DFSP (or other DFSP-related tumours) {19890351}. Giant cell fibroblastoma more frequently shows a balanced translocation without amplification than does DFSP (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/33/46\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans</em>).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-02-19T15:46:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58500,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Grossly, giant cell fibroblastoma is an ill-defined, infiltrative lesion with greyish-yellow and mucoid cut surfaces. The size of the lesions ranges from 0.6 to 8&nbsp;cm, with a mean of 3&ndash;4&nbsp;cm {17349565; 2804904; 12502925}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58501,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Giant cell fibroblastoma is a mainly subcutaneous neoplasm with infiltration of the dermis and (more rarely) superficial skeletal muscle. Typical cases of giant cell fibroblastoma infiltrate the subcutis in honeycomb or parallel growth patterns, spare cutaneous adnexal structures, and show variable cellularity; however, hypocellular areas with myxoid or collagenous stroma often predominate. The neoplasms are composed of bland spindled and stellate tumour cells and scattered multinucleated giant cells with a wreath-like arrangement of nuclei, which also line irregularly branching pseudovascular spaces. Mitoses are rare, and areas of tumour necrosis are usually absent. In about 15% of cases, areas of conventional DFSP are present (hybrid cases) {17349565; 2804904; 12502925}. Rarely, cases contain pigmented tumour cells, show prominent myxoid change, or contain fibrosarcomatous areas (representing tumour progression) {17349565}. By immunohistochemistry, spindle-shaped cells and multinucleated tumour giant cells are positive for CD34 {17349565; 12502925}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58502,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58503,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>COL1A1</em>-<em>PDGFB</em> fusion may be detected by various molecular approaches but is not generally required.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58504,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> infiltrative margins within dermis and subcutis; hypocellular lesion with myxoid or collagenous stroma; bland spindled and stellate cells and scattered multinucleated giant cells, often lining pseudovascular spaces.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> <em>COL1A1</em>-<em>PDGFB</em> fusion (in selected cases).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58505,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58506,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A local recurrence rate of about 50% has been reported {2804904}; however, if widely excised, giant cell fibroblastoma locally recurs only rarely {12502925}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:23:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]